            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for good value for money, then the Mandarin for to turn bad (of the weather)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: shōurù",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "shōurù — \"income\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "yīnwèi tā de shōurù bù gāo suǒyǐ tā měitiān zài jiā zuòfàn — His income isn't high, so he cooks at home every day."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Its characters come later; for now it is a word for the ear."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "shōurù is built from shōu 'to gather' and rù 'to go in', both inherited from Middle Chinese, syuw and nyip (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription), which glosses the second 'to take in'."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
